Google Cloud Shell itself is free for normal usage. The confusion usually comes from Google Cloud services used from Cloud Shell, which can cost money.

💻 Cloud Shell itself

Google provides a Cloud Shell environment at no additional charge for users. It gives you a temporary VM-like environment with tools such as:

Linux terminal
gcloud
Python
Git
Basic storage/home directory

So:

Cloud Shell = Free ✅

=========

💰 But the commands you run can use paid services

For example:

Cloud Shell
    ↓
Python program
    ↓
Vertex AI
    ↓
Gemini

Cloud Shell → generally free
Vertex AI / Gemini API usage → may consume your Google Cloud credits and can become billable depending on the service/model and billing status.

Similarly:

Cloud Shell
    ↓
Cloud Storage

Storage can have charges depending on usage.

========

Why did you see a billing/free-trial page?

Because Cloud Shell is part of Google Cloud, and Google Cloud uses a billing account for many services.

Your Free Trial gives you credits that can be used for eligible Google Cloud services.

Think of it like:

🏠 Cloud Shell = free workspace
🛠️ Google Cloud services = tools you may pay for depending on usage

For your current project

You successfully ran:

python test_gemini.py

and Gemini returned a response.

The Cloud Shell environment wasn't what consumed your main credit; the Vertex AI/Gemini service usage is the part you should pay attention to.

Also, keep an eye on your Google Cloud Billing / Credits page so you can see whether your usage is consuming the trial credit.

